Try-with-resources — safe cleanup.

Section three ended on a promise: the finally-to-close-a-resource pattern is so common, and so error-prone, that Java gave it dedicated syntax. That syntax is try-with-resources, and it's the correct, modern way to work with anything that must be closed — files, streams, sockets, database connections. It writes the finally for you, and it gets the hard parts right that hand-written code usually gets wrong.

Consider the pattern it replaces. Closing a resource by hand is deceptively tricky. You open a buffered reader, then in a try you read a line, and in a finally you call close. But you have to remember that finally — and what if close itself also throws? And with two resources, it nests into a pyramid, where each level is another chance to leak.

The new syntax is simple: you declare the resource in parentheses, right after the try keyword. Java closes it automatically at the end of the block — on a normal exit or an exception, with no finally needed. So try, open-paren, buffered reader r equals files-dot-new-buffered-reader of path, close-paren — then read a line inside. And r-dot-close is called at the end, guaranteed, on every path. You can declare several resources, separated by semicolons, and they close in reverse order of opening — which is the correct order for nested resources. So if you open an input stream and then an output stream, and transfer one to the other, the output is closed first, then the input.

What makes something a resource? The answer is the auto-closeable interface. The magic isn't specific to files. Any object whose class implements auto-closeable — which is just a single close method — can go in the parentheses. Streams, readers, connection, scanner, and any type of your own. That's the whole contract: implement auto-closeable, and your type works with try-with-resources for free. So a session class that implements auto-closeable, with a close method that releases something, gets closed automatically.

Now the subtle win: suppressed exceptions. Here's the correctness bug that hand-written finally almost always has. Suppose the try body throws — and then close also throws, while cleaning up. Which exception wins? In a manual finally, the close exception replaces the original — so you lose the real error, and see only the cleanup failure. Try-with-resources gets this right. The original exception propagates, and the close exception is attached to it as a suppressed exception — visible in the trace under the label "suppressed", and retrievable through get-suppressed. So the real failure stays primary, and nothing is silently lost.

Why is this the default now? Because try-with-resources isn't just a convenience — it's a correctness upgrade you should reach for by reflex. First, no leaks: close is guaranteed on every exit path, including the ones you forgot to think about. Second, less code, read top-down: the resource's lifetime is exactly the block, and there's no finally to scan for. And third, the right exception semantics: the primary failure survives, while cleanup failures are suppressed, not swapped in.

So the rule is simple: if it's auto-closeable, open it in a try-with-resources. Reserve a bare finally for cleanup that isn't a closeable resource. And this is exactly what makes the file API in the rest of the module safe to use — every stream and channel you're about to open is auto-closeable.
